    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, i thought APA 7th changed something about the first citation? i remember seeing something about using et al. right from the start even for the first time. can you clarify that part for me? also can you give me a quick example of how it would look in the actual sentence for both styles?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right; APA 7th edition did change that! For sources with three or more authors, APA 7th now requires you to use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" for *every* citation, including the very first one. In the context of a sentence, it would look like this: (Smith et al., 2020). In Chicago author-date, it often follows a similar pattern, but if you are using footnotes, you would list all names in the first footnote. For example, a footnote might read: ¹John Smith, Jane Doe, Bob Green, etc., *Title* (Year), page number.",
